Conduct Disorder
Paediatric & Neonatal
Aggression toward people/animals, Intentional property destruction, Chronic deceitfulness/rule-breaking

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-013
Reason for Visit: Acute behavioral crisis involving severe physical aggression and intentional property destruction requiring immediate assessment to rule out acute substance intoxication or organic psychosis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, parents, and local law enforcement (waiting outside)
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, violent escalation with the patient intentionally destroying significant household property and demonstrating physical aggression toward caregivers. Immediate assessment was required to evaluate for acute sympathomimetic toxidrome, secure the physical safety of the household, and determine if involuntary psychiatric scheduling was required prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He deliberately set fire to his brother's bed, punched me when I stopped him, and he simply doesn't care."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents following an acute episode of deliberate, intentional, and highly planned destruction of significant personal property, including distinct fire-setting tonight. Caregivers report a chronic, deeply repetitive, and pervasive pattern of intentional violation of the basic rights of others. The patient displays physical aggression explicitly directed toward family members and pets, completely lacking any genuine subsequent remorse. There is a history of chronic deceitfulness, frequent severe physical altercations, and chronic truancy from school commencing before age thirteen. The patient exhibits a profound lack of emotional empathy, resulting in distinct callous, unemotional core personality traits.
Functional Status: Physically intact, highly oppositional.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent danger to household members due to fire-setting and physical violence. Involvement of juvenile justice system.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Alert, guarded, physically tense, poor eye contact, displaying a flat and callous affect.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals normal pupillary size and reactivity. Sclerae are white with no conjunctival injection or nystagmus. Motor tone and reflexes are normal. No tremors or diaphoresis. Heart rate is regular without tachycardia, effectively ruling out acute stimulant intoxication. The diagnostic tetrad of aggression, property destruction, and chronic rule-breaking is heavily documented in the clinical history and observed via the destroyed environment.
Environmental Observations: Broken glass on the floor, scorch marks on a mattress, strong smell of smoke in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: No prescribed psychotropic medications. Patient denies illicit drug use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Conduct Disorder - Status: Acute behavioral crisis
Differentials:

Acute Substance Intoxication (e.g., Amphetamines) - Ruled out due to normal pupillary response, normal resting heart rate, lack of diaphoresis, and absence of pressured speech.

Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD) - Ruled out due to the presence of severe physical aggression toward people/animals and deliberate destruction of property (fire-setting), which strictly exceed the criteria for ODD.

First-Episode Psychosis - Ruled out due to the absence of auditory or visual hallucinations, disorganized thought processes, or delusional beliefs driving the behavior.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Imminent risk of severe harm to others)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s acute presentation of severe violence and fire-setting mandated an immediate after-hours assessment alongside law enforcement to ensure physical safety and medical clearance. Telephone triage identified the critical need to definitively rule out acute substance-induced delirium or an organic psychotic break driving the extreme behavior. Objective clinical evaluation confirmed an intact neurological baseline without sympathomimetic signs, confirming the behavior as an acute manifestation of severe Conduct Disorder rather than an acute medical toxidrome. Delaying this assessment would have left the caregivers in imminent physical danger and risked lethal house fires. Immediate intervention established medical clearance, allowing for appropriate psychiatric and law enforcement management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Medical clearance provided. No acute pharmacological sedation indicated or safely administerable in the community setting. De-escalation facilitated with the presence of law enforcement.
Positive Behavioral Change: Establish absolute, non-negotiable physical boundaries with immediate, predetermined consequences. If the patient engages in any physical violence or property destruction, caregivers must immediately remove themselves to a pre-arranged safe room and contact emergency services. This removes any secondary gain of interpersonal conflict and enforces strict legal consequences for violent behavior.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to child and adolescent psychiatric services and coordination with juvenile justice.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fire extinguishers recommended for the home. Removal of all accelerants and lighters.
Follow-up: Mandated psychiatric evaluation within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via law enforcement or call 000 if the patient produces a weapon, attempts further fire-setting, threatens lethal violence, or if caregivers feel their lives are in immediate danger.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis requires comprehensive, structured psychiatric evaluation; acute interventions prioritize safety.
Clinical Course: Without intensive, multi-systemic therapy and secure boundaries, the behavior carries a high risk of progression to antisocial personality disorder in adulthood.

